 is use  tumor organoids  and these are growing in popularity  because they're a great model system  to measure single cell response within a  patient mapped  sample so what we do is we get a biopsy  or a surgical sample  from a patient and we just take the  tissue and we  mechanically dissociate it that means we  cut it up with uh scissors  and then we embed it in a 3d collagen  matrix so it's kind of like a jello  you know looking thing gelatin kind of  and then we put media over the top so  what we're trying to do here  is mimic the body and try to fool these  tumor cells into behaving as they would  in the body  and the benefit here for us as  microscopists is that these are small  things so these tumor organoids are only  about  200 sometimes they get bigger few  hundred microns in diameter  so we can assess them with things like  two photon microscopy and we can image  all the way through  the tumor organoids in this way and so  this  advancements in tumor organoids have  been really beneficial for us  in assessing single cell heterogeneity  and previous groups have really done a  fantastic job characterizing these tumor  organoids it's a  popular area of study and they've shown  that genetic expression levels in the  organoids closely  mimics the host tumor the heterogeneity  and cell types of the host tumor  maintained in the organoids  and there are some other studies looking  at functional imaging within these tumor  organoids and  i'm happy to see this is a growing area  of investigation  because i think it's a great way that we  can contribute as  imaging experts to this great need in  oncology  so in my lab we're trying to do things  sort of on  two ends of the spectrum we're thinking  about clinical impact so we want to  develop  high throughput drug screening  technologies on a single patient level  so that we can inform clinical treatment  decisions because as you know each tumor  is different each patient responds  differently to treatment  so these