ules  it's very sensitive again to malignancy  and drug response  so as i mentioned before we're using two  photon microscopy  and we're using time correlated single  photon counting so we're building up  this decay curve for each pixel in the  image  and then we fit this decay curve um  to a two exponential model because we're  looking at both the free  and bound forms of nadh and fad we also  have this instrument response function  that we first deconvolve with the  measured values  to obtain our decay curse so  i'm going to mostly talk about the mean  lifetime today  and that's the weighted average of the  short and the long lifetime so this  alpha value is just  the contribution of the free nadh or the  alpha 2 is the contribution of  the protein bound nadh and that's what  we're measuring in our experiments  we're also doing single cell analysis so  again this is autofluorescence  measurements  so we had to develop our own pipelines  for  segmenting cells specifically cell  cytoplasm so we're saying many out the  nucleus  and keeping the cytosol and mitochondria  all together  and through this we can then graph  basically the number of cells and an  autofluorescence variable and then start  to ask questions about the heterogeneity  and those populations  so i'm actually going to breeze over a  bunch of our work in just one slide here  we've done a bunch of work in vivo  and in tumor organoids derived from both  animal models and patients  and we've shown that we can combine all  of these variables in a way that makes a  little easier to understand what the  cell is thinking  so i already told you about the redox  ratio and the lifetimes of nadh and fad  which have a two exponential decay  we just did this empirically so this is  from a multivariate  analysis of variance we knew which cells  were responsive to treatment and which  weren't across multiple model systems  and we found that basically a linear  combination of the redox ratio  nadh mean lifetime and fad mean lifetime  all mean normalized with the  minus one coefficient here for the fab  provided us with really sensitive and  specific information  on cell response to treatment so  biochemically this is not as  meaningful as each variable on its own  because it's composite  but within this we know that we're  looking at the relative amounts of  electron donor and acceptor in a cell  and the enzyme activity of nadh and fad  and we define this omi index to increase  or not change  in cells that are resistant to treatment  or to decrease in cells that are  responsive to treatment  so we showed that depending on the model  system of somewhere around one to three  days post-treatment we're very sensitive  to treatment response  in organoids that predicts later in vivo  response  in mouse models and in patient samples  so for the clinical translation i really  want to highlight my clinical  collaborators dusty deming and mark  burkhard  and this fantastic program they've made  the precision medicine molecular tumor  board  so this is a wisconsin service it's  supported by the wisconsin state  government  and it is um a free referral service so  you can refer yourself or any of your  you know doctors can refer you um and  what you get is a free  genomic report so we need a biopsy or a  surgical sample and we can look for  common genetic alterations and then  the smart people on the review board  like dusty and mark get together and  look at this genomic report and then  make a recommendation so they might find  that you have a pretty typical genomic  report and you should go  on standard treatment they might find  you have a particular mutation that  makes you a really good candidate for a  clinical trial and  they might find you have a mutation that  isn't common for your cancer but is  common in other cancers so you might use  a different cancer treatment  so we have tapped into this precision  medicine molecular tumor board and some  of the samples  in which we get a 