hat  this person would not do well on 5fu  treatment just based on the mean change  in the omi index  and on the heterogeneity of those  changes  so when we started these studies we  thought gee these little squiggles of  you know population density modeling  here are really useful for understanding  what's happening in the patient and  perhaps we should quantify  how these squiggles change with patients  oh i forgot to mention we also measure  you know standard measures of response  like ki 67 which is a  immunohistochemical measure of  proliferation  and clear caspase-3 which is also  immunohistochemistry of apoptosis and  we see that we have good correlation  between you know some of these like ks67  but it's not perfect um and so we're  measuring something related to  proliferation but not exactly the same  and not exactly the same as apoptosis so  it's a  useful measure but different from what  we're used to seeing  so the heterogeneity index is something  we developed  to try to understand the single cell  metabolic response that we were  observing in  our patient derived samples and we  actually  um developed this from the shannon  diversity index which is used in  information theory it's used in ecology  to understand diversity  in an ecosystem we modified it a little  bit so that it could  characterize the changes we saw and the  samples we were looking at  so really we're just looking at uh the  number of subpopulations in the sample  so one with one subpopulation is more  homogeneous than  two subpopulations equality in  sub-populations means it's more  heterogeneous  instead of like say a minority very  small sub-population  and a majority very large sub-population  a greater separation between these  sub-populations also  indicates greater heterogeneity and then  we also included a standard deviation  measure  so that we could define how broad these  subpopulations are  so overall we define this as a weighted  heterogeneity index because we  decided to 