 originating so we can capture that  with our techniques so what exactly are  we  measuring we're measuring  autofluorescence from two metabolic  coenzymes  so nadh and fad are  ubiquitous we even find them in bacteria  so they're  very common um electron donor and  acceptor  they're involved for example nadh is  involved in greater than 300 reactions  in the cell both in the cysosol and in  the mitochondria  and we're just highlighting a few here  to give you an idea of what we're  looking at so  first of all nadh and nadph are  spectrally overlapping and they have  overlapping fluorescent  properties so we refer to them  collectively as nadph  and fad and fmn are also closely related  in their spectral properties so this is  a combination of both of those  molecules so you can see we produce you  know  nadh and consume nadh in various  metabolic reactions  um and what we do is take the ratio of  nadh to fad  and call this the optical redox ratio  which provides us  information on how oxidized or reduced  the overall  cell is and this has become very um  useful in many different studies for  measuring whether a tumor is malignant  or not whether it's aggressive or not  and we use it to measure drug response  we're also measuring the fluorescence  lifetime of these molecules so that's  the time  that the molecule is in the excited  state before decaying back down to the  ground state  and this is useful because nadh and fad  have two very distinct conformations so  for example nadh when it's free it folds  up on itself like this  and when it binds to a protein it  extends and that creates  two very distinct lifetimes due to some  self-crunching here  so the free nadh has a short lifetime  the protein bound nadh has a long  lifetime  and the converse is true for fad so when  we measure the fluorescence lifetime  we're really measuring  the pools of nadh or fad that are in a  free  or a protein bound state and this gives  us an idea of binding activity of these  molecules  it's very sensitive again to malignancy  and drug response  so as i mentioned before we're using two  photon microscopy  and we're using time correlated single  photon counting so we're building up  this decay curve for each pixel in the  image  and then we fit this decay curve um  to a two exponential model because we're  looking at both the free  and bound forms of nadh and fad we also  have this instrument response function  that we first deconvolve with the  measured values  to obtain our decay curse so  i'm going to mostly talk about the mean  lifetime today  and that's the weighted average of the  short and the long lifetime so this  alpha value is just  the contribution of the free nadh or the  alpha 2 is the contribution of  the protein bound nadh and that's what  we're measuring in our experiments  we're also doing single cell analysis so  again this is autofluorescence  measurements  so we had to develop our own pipelines  for  segmenting cells specifically cell  cytoplasm so we're saying many out the  nucleus  and keeping the cytosol and mitochondria  all together  and through this we can then graph  basically the number of cells and an  autofluorescence variable and then start  to ask questions about the heterogeneity  and those populations  so i'm actually going to breeze over a  bunch of our work in just one slide here  we've done a bunch of work in vivo  and in tumor organoids derived from both  animal models and patients  and we've shown that we can combine all  of these variables in a way that makes a  little easier to understand what the  cell is thinking  so i already told you about the redox  ratio and the lifetimes of nadh and fad  which have a two exponential decay  we just did this empirically so this is  from a multivariate  analysis of variance we knew which cells  were responsive to treatment and which  weren't across multiple model systems  and we found that basically a linear  combination of the redox ratio  nadh mean lifetime and fad mean lifetime  all mean normalized with the  minus one coefficient here for the fab  provided us with really sensitive and  specific information  on cell response to treatment so  biochemically this is not as  meaningful as each variable on its own  because it's composite  but within this we know that we're  looking at the relative amounts of  electron donor and acceptor in a cell  and the enzyme activity of nadh and fad  and we define this omi index to increase  or not change  in cells that are resistant to treatment  or to decrease in cells that are  responsive to treatment  so we showed that depending on the model  system of somewhere around one to three  days post-treatment we're very sensitive  to treatment response  in organoids that predicts later in vivo  response  in mouse models and in patient samples  so for the clinical translation i really  want to highlight my clinical  collaborators dusty deming and mark  burkhard  and this fantastic program they've made  the precision medicine molecular tumor  board  so this is a wisconsin service it's  supported by the wisconsin state  government  and it is um a free referral service so  you can refer yourself or any of your  you know doctors can refer you um and  what you get is a free  genomic report so we need a biopsy or a  surgical sample and we can look for  common genetic alterations and then  the smart people on the review board  like dusty and mark get together and  look at this genomic report and then  make a recommendation so they might find  that you have a pretty typical genomic  report and you should go  on standard treatment they might find  you have a particular mutation that  makes you a really good candidate for a  clinical trial and  they might find you have a mutation that  isn't common for your cancer but is  common in other cancers so you might use  a different cancer treatment  so we have tapped into this precision  medicine molecular tumor board and some  of the samples  in which we get a genomic report we also  grow  tumor organoids and we ask questions  about whether we can predict which  treatment is best and what alternative  treatments might be best  by testing those treatments directly on  the patient samples  so i'm going to talk first about some  pancreatic cancer studies we've done  and then a little bit about some breast  cancer studies we've done but we have  ongoing studies and multiple cancer  types  so for example we have here a pancreatic  cancer patient  and the control is just given vehicle  and the  g plus f is gem cytamine and full  furanox that's a common treatment for  pancreatic cancer  and these are both organoids from a  patient uh the same patient  so they're matched and what we found is  that this patient would respond well to  the combination treatment  and they have a nice sort of homogeneous  distribution of cellular metabolism in  the control samples  and a nice homogeneous distribution of  cell metabolism in the treated samples  so this to us  looks like maybe someone who would  respond well to treatment  here is another patient patient eight um  they were prescribed five fu treatment  so i should mention the first patient  was prescribed this gem cytomine and  fulfill fulfilling ox treatment same  story here we have the control  cells and the treated cells in 3d  culture  so all the cells we're looking at here  are tumor cells  and what we see is a very low  difference between the omi index and the  control and the five fu treated  organoids  and this glasses delta is just a measure  of mean differences with respect to  standard deviation so it's a little more  strict than what you'll find from a  t-test  and what we see in the control samples  is that this is a heterogeneous response  even  a heterogeneous distribution of  metabolic activity even without  any treatment and when we do treat the  patient  sample we see a very similar  heterogeneous distribution that has not  changed  with treatment so we would predict that  this person would not do well on 5fu  treatment just based on the mean change  in the omi index  and on the heterogeneity of those  changes  so when we started these studies we  thought gee these little squiggles of  you know population density modeling  here are really useful for understanding  what's happening in the patient and  perhaps we should quantify  how these squiggles change with patients  oh i forgot to mention we also measure  you know standard measures of response  like ki 67 which is a  immunohistochemical measure of  proliferation  and clear caspase-3 which is also  immunohistochemistry of apoptosis and  we see that we have good correlation  between you know some of these like ks67  but it's not perfect um and so we're  measuring something related to  proliferation but not exactly the same  and not exactly the same as apoptosis so  it's a  useful measure but different from what  we're used to seeing  so the heterogeneity index is something  we developed  to try to understand the single cell  metabolic response that we were  observing in  our patient derived samples and we  actually  um developed this from the shannon  diversity index which is used in  information theory it's used in ecology  to understand diversity  in an ecosystem we modified it a little  bit so that it could  characterize the changes we saw and the  samples we were looking at  so really we're just looking at uh the  number of subpopulations in the sample  so one with one subpopulation is more  homogeneous than  two subpopulations equality in  sub-populations means it's more  heterogeneous  instead of like say a minority very  small sub-population  and a majority very large sub-population  a greater separation between these  sub-populations also  indicates greater heterogeneity and then  we also included a standard deviation  measure  so that we could define how broad these  subpopulations are  so overall we define this as a weighted  heterogeneity index because we  decided to add this sort of standard  deviation at the end  so it's a little different than the  shannon diversity index but it provides  similar information  so what we found in this initial study  of just  see one two three four five six seven  patients um  is that we had some who responded much  better to treatment than others so  uh the the ones we predicted to respond  are in blue  um there are actual responses indicated  at the end here  and red are those that we predicted  would not respond well to the treatment  they were prescribed  so we just arbitrarily more or less drew  this line here at 12 months which  discriminates those patients that we  predicted would respond versus those  patients we  predicted would not respond there's many  ways to design this study and this is  very small  so um we defined whether or not these  patients would respond or not based on  two things  this class index um delta which i'm  showing here the definition again is the  omi index for the control minus that of  the drug treated sample  divided by the standard deviation of  control so we tested multiple treatments  but here we're just talking about the  treatment that the patient  was actually given and then we follow  them up over time  that's what this swimmer plot's about so  you can see that the  predicted patient non-responders had a  much lower  change in their omi index compared to  those we predicted would respond to  treatment so that checks out with how we  define our omi index  in our previous studies we also  quantified the weighted heterogeneity  index which i defined here again for you  and we find that those patients who  responded to treatments they might have  had very heterogeneous tumors to begin  with  but those got much less heterogeneous  with time  or sorry with treatment uh whereas these  patients who had  a very poor response had high head  originating that did not change much  with treatment  or low heterogeneity that then increased  with treatments  so this is the first study of course and  we're looking at more patients  through the precision medicine molecular  tumor board to see if this holds  i mentioned the only response to the  tumor cells so far so  organoids are great in that they also  have other cell types so we see  stellate cells or fibroblasts growing in  the culture too  and especially adhering to the glass so  that's what we're seeing here  are the fibroblasts in these patient  derived samples  so we quantified response for these  cells as well in the hopes of finding  drugs that might target these important  cells within  pancreatic cancer so actually still  eight cells and fibroblasts can make up  as much as  80 or 90 of the tumor mass so you can  see here some of these  fibroblasts are responding very well to  the treatment in a homogeneous way  um some others are not responding very  well and  are very heterogeneous and then others  are responding well also in a  homogeneous way  so you can see that we have um you know  the middle patient here who has  the least response in their fiber blocks  had the earliest recurrence  whereas these two which responded well  to their treatment  um had longer um  life so that so far was just talking  about pancreatic cancer but we also have  some studies in breast cancer  so i wanted to show just one sort of  representative slide from breast cancer  because  this study relied only on  core needle biopsies which are very  small samples but they're valuable  samples because the core needle biopsies  are taken before neoadjuvant treatment  so then  we have a very well defined treatment  window of neoadjuvant and then surgery  so we can look at exactly how those  patients respond to treatment  so here we're using different drugs  because we are looking at breast cancer  paclitaxel on its own uh with  cyclophosphamide  and with cyclophosphamide and  doxorubicin so we looked at a few  other um drugs depending on what the  patient was expressing for example if  they had a her2-positive tumor  we gave him trustus med um but you can  see here very clearly that the  combination treatment  created the largest changes in omi index  in the  patient drive tumor organized if we just  look at a couple of examples this is one  patient who had a very  good response to their prescribed  treatment in their organoids and it was  very homogeneous response  and at the time of surgery the  pathologist indicated that this patient  had minimum residual disease that agrees  well with  these large changes we saw in their  organoids  here we have another patient who  actually had extensive  residual disease at the time of surgery  when we looked at their  tumor organoids for their prescribed  treatment they had  smaller glasses delta it's a smaller  change in omi index with treatment  and they had a more heterogeneous tumor  before treatment so again this is only a  few patients and we're trying to  put all this data together but that  gives us an indication of what might be  the path for breast cancer assessments  especially in the neoadjuvant setting  so i just mentioned today a few patients  from pancreatic cancer and breast cancer  and i really only talked about the  clinical studies we have  many more studies in mouse models of  cancer too  so we're trying to reconcile what's  happening in patients with what we see  in mice and we're trying to understand  what particular parameters  are most predictive of treatment  response we're looking  as well at oral cancer neuroendocrine  tumors and colon cancers  and this is all again through the  precision medicine molecular tumor board  at the university of wisconsin in  madison  so today i talked a little bit about  single cell adaptation in  cancer and hopefully i've convinced you  that this is an important feature to  look at  both pre-clinically for drug development  and clinically for informing  patient treatment decisions and we  looked at a few examples of  patient-matched organoids  in both pancreatic cancer and breast  cancer  to understand whether cellular metabolic  heterogeneity can  explain some of our treatment response  in the clinic  and we're hoping that we could use this  method to predict patient treatment  response and develop more effective  therapies for patients  i'd like to thank especially joe sharik  who did  all the work in the breast cancer and  pancreatic cancer  my collaborators of course um dusty  deming mark percard  sherry patch and all the wonderful  people i work with at the university of  wisconsin  and i'm happy to take any questions and  thank you  hi kyle how are you  well thank you melissa uh for that talk  and let's move on to the q a  portion um so we have a question about  image analysis we can maybe start with  that  for uh segmentation of single nuclei  in the uh organoid structure you  mentioned that you're using ai  could you talk a little bit more about  this in in detail  yeah i actually um we're not really  using ai so we're using cell profiler  which is a  free wear you know out of the broad  institute  um and it is um  you know identifying an object which is  the nuclei and then  once we get to the nuclei it propagates  out until it identifies the um  cell border so it's not it's not ai  but we are working on more methods  because um  it isn't perfect and we have to go in  there and fix stuff often  so we're also looking at um  convolutional neural networks and  you know we're getting those to work  real well in cells that are sparse  but as soon as they start to get close  and start to touch like they do in  organoids you know we're having some  trouble so  we're running the cnns and then adding  more on top of that um  but it's not perfect yet so we're still  relying on the cell profiler pipelines  so moving on to some questions that we  have about  the the culture conditions is there  selection pressure for the cells which  grow out  under the organic conditions that you  provide and do all the  cells in the tumor sample grow up yeah  that's a super cool question because  it actually speaks to one of the  advantages of organoid so what we find  when we do like genetic hot spot  sequencing on the organoids is that we  have much cleaner data  than we do from the original tumor  sample and that is because we do select  for  tumor cells within the culture rather  than um  a large degree of stromal cells mostly  is what we're keeping out  just based on the culture conditions and  then further what we're finding i wish i  had the image to show you is that  if you look at a plate of organoids and  you look at say the redox ratio of them  you'll see some  that are much lower than others and you  can actually pipette them out  and then sequence those and you'll find  that those are the subclones  um so it's a really cool way to be able  to  identify subclones and then actually  isolate them and then ask how they  differ  i think i answered the question i kind  of went off the rails there yeah  so we have yeah a few uh sort of  clarification questions  when you perform the assays and  treatments do you keep the organizer  tumorides within the ecm or remove them  from the ecm  uh yes we do remove them from the ecm  and then we put them in a matrigel or  collagen matrix depending on the  protocol  most people are using matrigel which i  know  isn't fantastic because you know that's  the batch variation and it has all kinds  of unknowns in it but that's what is the  current standard protocol  um we do try to keep you know with  culture conditions we can keep t cells  in there you just have to add cytokine  um you can keep fibroblasts i showed you  um  and other cell types will grow in there  you just have to have the right things  in your media if you want those  yeah okay so one looks like we have  time for one last question um how  quickly can you scale the organized from  the time that you receive them from the  patients  to conducting these these assays ah  right so the time i think we um  used to just get the biopsy and then  within three days  we would like we would treat them almost  immediately and then two days later  assess treatment response  uh but working with some other  collaborators they prefer to passage the  organoid so they might wait seven to ten  days before giving the drug  and then the stuffing responds so again  it depends on what your goals  are um if you want to retain more of the  original tumor cells or if you want to  select for those more  um pure population  well thank you very much melissa for the  talk and answering uh these questions  and thank you all for participating  yeah fun stuff thank you
 

